{
  "answers": [
    {
      "question_id": "Q1",
      "question": "What event or action officially signals that a meeting has ended and that the summarization process should begin?",
      "answer": "The process kicks off once the meeting wraps up and the person responsible for capturing notes or a transcript has that raw material in hand. Collecting those notes or the recorded transcript is the first concrete step, so possession of that content effectively marks the start of the summarization workflow. The specific trigger event is not further defined beyond that."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q2",
      "question": "How is the meeting summary currently created — for example, is it written manually by a participant, produced by a dedicated note-taker, or generated using a transcription or AI tool?",
      "answer": "A designated note-taker handles the work: gathering the raw notes or transcript, pulling out key decisions and follow-up tasks, clarifying anything ambiguous with the people who own those decisions, and then composing the summary. Automation tools could assist with extracting decisions and drafting the write-up, but the note-taker role drives the process."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q3",
      "question": "What specific sections or elements are required in a meeting summary, such as decisions made, action items with owners, agenda topics covered, or attendee list?",
      "answer": "The summary must capture the decisions reached, the follow-up tasks along with who is responsible for each, and any supporting materials linked or attached. The source inputs include the original agenda, the notes or transcript, the list of who attended, and the compiled task list. Those elements shape what the finished summary must contain."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q4",
      "question": "How are the recipients of the summary email determined — is it automatically taken from the meeting attendee list, a predefined group, or manually selected each time?",
      "answer": "The workflow references a group of \"relevant recipients\" but does not specify exactly how that list is compiled — whether it is pulled automatically from attendee records, chosen from a standing distribution group, or picked manually each time. That detail is not defined in the process description."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q5",
      "question": "Before the summary email is sent, does anyone need to review or approve the content, and if so, who holds that responsibility?",
      "answer": "No formal approval or review gate is described before the email goes out. The note-taker does check with decision owners to resolve unclear items before drafting, but once the draft is ready and supporting materials are attached, the note-taker sends it directly without a separate sign-off step."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q6",
      "question": "Is there a defined time limit within which the summary email must be sent after the meeting concludes, and what happens if that deadline is missed?",
      "answer": "No specific time limit for sending the summary email is defined in the process, and no consequences for a late send are described. This detail is not specified."
    }
  ]
}
